          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe as secondary prevention after a heart attack?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes secondary prevention as the measures used for someone who already has coronary artery disease, such as a previous heart attack, a procedure to open the arteries, or visible narrowing on a scan. The aim is to lower the chance of another heart event. The source is *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis* (major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary).\n\nAccording to that document, secondary prevention is a package of measures used together, not one treatment. It notes that partial use is the common failure, and that non-drug elements are often the ones dropped. The passage it returned names two components:\n- **Antiplatelet therapy:** long-term single antiplatelet treatment for most people, with a set period of dual antiplatelet treatment after a heart attack or stent placement. The length is individualized against bleeding risk.\n- **High-intensity statin therapy.**\n\nThe retrieved text cuts off before listing the remaining components, so I can't tell you the full list from these sources. I also can't give any doses or name products to use.\n\nThe results did not contain anything specific about heart attack follow-up or lifestyle measures. The blood-pressure and diabetes passages that came back are about those conditions in general, not about secondary prevention after a heart attack, so I have not used them here. A cardiologist or primary care clinician can explain which parts of this package apply to a particular person.\n\nIf you or someone near you has chest pain, pressure, or trouble breathing right now, call emergency services immediately. The search tool also returned an emergency notice with this answer. Your question did not describe an emergency, so I have not treated it as one, but if any symptoms are happening now, don't wait.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
